  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every real kappa, c, r0 and every sequence eta: N -> R, assume kappa >= 1, 0 < c < 1/4, r0 >= 0, and 0 < eta(n) <= c for every natural n. Natural numbers include zero, and real reciprocals are totalized with 0^(-1) = 0. Define K(x) = (1+x)/(kappa^(-1)+x) for real x; tau(x) = K(x) if K(x) >= 2, and tau(x) = c otherwise; a(x,h) = (h+tau(x))/(1+x); and step(x,h) = x+a(x,h)^(-1). Define r: N -> R by r(0) = r0 and r(j+1) = step(r(j),eta(j)) for every natural j. Then all of the following hold simultaneously: (1) For every natural n, r(n) >= 0 and r(n+1) > 0. (2) For every natural m,n with m <= n, K(r(n)) <= K(r(m)). (3) For every natural m,n with m <= n, if K(r(m)) < 2, then K(r(n)) < 2. (4) For every natural M,N, if (4/5)^M K(r0) < 2, then 0 < r(M+1+N)^(-1) <= 2c (2c/(1+2c))^N. (5) For every real B > 0, there exists a natural J > 0 with 0 < r(J)^(-1) <= B. All numerical fractions in these formulas are real, and powers have natural exponents. No positivity of r(0) is assumed: if r0 = 0 its reciprocal equals zero; reciprocal positivity is asserted at all the displayed strictly positive indices.",
